DDEESSCCRRIIPPTTIIOONN
     The ssaavveeccoorree utility copies a core dump into _d_i_r_e_c_t_o_r_y, or the current
     working directory if no _d_i_r_e_c_t_o_r_y argument is given, and enters a reboot
     message and information about the core dump into the system log.

     The options are as follows:

     ----lliibbxxoo      Generate output via libxo(3) in a selection of different hu‐
                  man and machine readable formats.  See xo_parse_args(3) for
                  details on command line arguments.

     --CC           Check to see if a dump exists, and display a brief message
                  to indicate the status.  An exit status of 0 indicates that
                  a dump is there, 1 indicates that none exists.  This option
                  is compatible only with the [--vv] option.

     --cc           Clear the dump, so that future invocations of ssaavveeccoorree will
                  ignore it.

     --ff           Force a dump to be taken even if either the dump was cleared
                  or if the dump header information is inconsistent.

     --kk           Do not clear the dump after saving it.

     --LL           Instruct ssaavveeccoorree to generate and save a kernel dump of the
                  running system, rather than copying one from a dump device.

     --mm _m_a_x_d_u_m_p_s  Maximum number of dumps to store.  Once the number of stored
                  dumps is equal to _m_a_x_d_u_m_p_s the counter will restart from 0.

     --uu           Uncompress the dump in case it was compressed by the kernel.

     --vv           Print out some additional debugging information.  Specify
                  twice for more information.

     --ZZ           Compress the dump (see zstd(1)).  This option is only sup‐
                  ported in conjunction with the --LL option.  Regular dumps can
                  be configured for compression with zstd using dumpon(8).

     --zz           Compress the dump (see gzip(1)).  The dump may already be
                  compressed if the kernel was configured to do so by
                  dumpon(8).  In this case, the option has no effect.

                  If used in conjunction with the --LL option, the requested
                  live dump will be compressed with gzip.

     The ssaavveeccoorree utility looks for dumps on each device specified by the
     _d_e_v_i_c_e argument(s), or on each device in _/_e_t_c_/_f_s_t_a_b marked as “dump” or
     “swap”.  The ssaavveeccoorree utility checks the core dump in various ways to
     make sure that it is complete.  If it passes these checks, it saves the
     core image in _d_i_r_e_c_t_o_r_y_/_v_m_c_o_r_e_._# and information about the core in
     _d_i_r_e_c_t_o_r_y_/_i_n_f_o_._#.  If the core is encrypted, it saves the dump key in
     _d_i_r_e_c_t_o_r_y_/_k_e_y_._#.  The core can be later decrypted using decryptcore(8).
     For kernel textdumps generated with the textdump(4) facility, output will
     be stored in the tar(5) format and named _d_i_r_e_c_t_o_r_y_/_t_e_x_t_d_u_m_p_._t_a_r_._#.  The
     “#” is the number from the first line of the file _d_i_r_e_c_t_o_r_y_/_b_o_u_n_d_s, and
     it is incremented and stored back into the file each time ssaavveeccoorree suc‐
     cessfully runs.

     The ssaavveeccoorree utility also checks the available disk space before attempt‐
     ing to make the copies.  If there is insufficient disk space in the file
     system containing _d_i_r_e_c_t_o_r_y, or if the file _d_i_r_e_c_t_o_r_y_/_m_i_n_f_r_e_e exists and
     the number of free kilobytes (for non-superusers) in the file system af‐
     ter the copies were made would be less than the number in the first line
     of this file, the copies are not attempted.

     If ssaavveeccoorree successfully copies the kernel and the core dump, the core
     dump is cleared so that future invocations of ssaavveeccoorree will ignore it.

     The ssaavveeccoorree utility is meant to be called near the end of the initial‐
     ization file _/_e_t_c_/_r_c (see rc(8)).

